Very interesting! The three canonical schemas DO share the same data class values: s > 5/2, delta in (1/2,1), spaces h - delta_ij in H^s_delta, K in H^{s-1}_{delta+1}. The map's G-FORM unmet item says "no single frozen data class (s,delta,norm) is shared by F1/F2a/F2b, which disables the licensed C0=>C2 transfer". At the CURRENT hashes (b65fcc0f, 8dae50da, a8d899d2), the data class appears shared — though with small textual differences:
- F1 spaces: "h - delta_ij in H^s_delta, K in H^{s-1}_{delta+1} (weighted Sobolev)"
- F2a/F2b spaces: "h - delta_ij in H^s_delta, K in H^{s-1}_{delta+1}"
- F1 has status "standard_choice; the numeric thresholds are UNVERIFIED citations, L1 owns verification", F2a/F2b "standard_choice; UNVERIFIED citation".
- F1 has "parity_conditions": "not imposed; imposing a parity condition would shrink the class and must be declared as a different class"; F2s say "not imposed".
- F2b admmass has extra "hypotheses_reconciliation" note.

So the map's unmet claim might be stale (referring to earlier revision hashes). This is a great bounded, falsifiable worker task: **measure whether the three canonical schemas at their current sha256 share one data-class triple (s, delta, norm)**, and if so, report the exact normalized triple and the residual second-order differences (e.g., parity_conditions annotation, status annotation, hypotheses_reconciliation note) that a strict "byte-identical frozen data class" requirement would still flag. This directly tests a G-FORM unmet item; result either way is valuable: if shared → the unmet item may be stale and the lead can update the gate; if not shared → pinpoint the mismatch down to the field.

